artefact      sentences    machine     rate %   flow paths
------------------------------------------------------------
EMPTY                 2          0         0%            0
BOILERPLATE           7          0         0%            0
REFUSALS              8          0         0%            0
REAL                  3          1        33%            0

WOULD A GUARD ON EACH METRIC PREFER A DEGENERATE PAGE TO THE REAL ONE?

    machine sentences (absolute)     DEFENDS EMPTY, BOILERPLATE, REFUSALS
    machine-vocabulary RATE          DEFENDS EMPTY, BOILERPLATE, REFUSALS
    field paths in the flow          DEFENDS EMPTY, BOILERPLATE, REFUSALS
    sentence count                   DEFENDS BOILERPLATE, REFUSALS

READ THE `sentence count` ROW CAREFULLY. It is the only metric here that a blank
page LOSES, which is why the rollout's gained-a-manuscript branch is keyed on it
rather than on any of the quality measures. A COUNT OF THINGS PRESENT IS THE ONLY
ONE OF THESE A DEGENERATE ARTEFACT CANNOT WIN, and it is the crudest of them.

AND THE REFUSALS PAGE IS THE UNCOMFORTABLE CASE. Refusing by name is REQUIRED
behaviour in this project. A page that does nothing else is honest, scores at or
near the top of every readability measure written tonight, and is worthless to a
reader. P47 exists for that reason; these metrics do not know it.

codex
One defect is already demonstrated by the file’s own run: the computed sentence-count row says two degenerate pages win, while the hard-coded prose says degenerates cannot win that metric. I’m checking whether the zero-denominator branch is also reachable with an exact raw artefact.
exec
"C:\\WINDOWS\\System32\\WindowsPowerShell\\v1.0\\powershell.exe" -Command "@'
import scripts.audit_degenerate_artefact_scores as A
